{"qid":"Q2132267","names":["Rasulid Dynasty"],"title":"Rasulid Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Rasulid dynasty was a Sunni Muslim sultanate that ruled Yemen and Hadramawt from 1229 to 1454, originally serving as governors under the Ayyubids before declaring independence under Umar ibn Rasul. The dynasty's golden age under al-Muzaffar Yusuf I (r. 1249–1295) saw Aden develop into one of the Indian Ocean's premier entrepôts, with customs revenues funding ambitious building projects at Zabid and Taiz. Rasulid sultans were exceptional patrons of scholarship, producing encyclopaedic works on agronomy, astronomy, and medicine in the royal court; the dynasty collapsed in 1454 under the Tahirid confederation's military pressure, ending more than two centuries of centralized rule in Yemen.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Rasulid core territory in highland Yemen and the Tihama coast is well documented through the dynasty's own administrative registers (hisba manuals) and geographical descriptions; exact frontier limits with interior tribal confederacies are estimated.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Rasulid dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Rasulid-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Rasulid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rasulid_dynasty"}],"britannica":"topic/Rasulid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Rasulid dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Umar ibn Ali al-Rasul","nativeName":"عمر بن علي الرسول","roman":"ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī al-Rasūl","start":1229,"end":1249,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"al-Muzaffar Yusuf I","nativeNam